ch foreman had argued, but some walks you take solo. a land deed was in the courthouse. This was about more than paper.\n\nthe railroad boss stepped out with two gunmen. 'Last chance,' the railroad boss said. 'Ride out.' Ada Coleman thought of the Rio Bravo valley, of the ranch foreman, of every small place that ever needed one person to stand. 'No,' Ada Coleman said."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Drive', 'text': "The cattle tested them: stampede at midnight, Ada Coleman riding the point with a lantern and a prayer; the river crossing, the ranch foreman swimming the lead steer; the long dry stretch, sharing the last canteen.\n\nThey reached the railhead with the herds and the deed and their heads high. the Rio Bravo valley was solvent. the Rio Bravo valley was free. Ada Coleman looked back at the dust of the trail and felt the peculiar ache of a job done.\n\nAda Coleman organized the drive: every ranch, every hand, moving the herds to the railhead themselves, cutting out the railroad boss's middlemen. the ranch foreman rode drag. a land deed — the deed — rode in Ada Coleman's breast pocket, the reason for it all."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'Home Range', 'text': "But Ada Coleman stayed through the winter, helping the ranch foreman mend fences, teaching the kids to read sign, being — for a while — part of the Rio Bravo valley instead of just its defender. It suited them.\n\nthe railroad boss was last seen heading east, smaller in the distance. the Rio Bravo valley didn't gloat. The frontier doesn't gloat; it just keeps the water clean and the word good.\n\na land deed stayed in the courthouse, under glass, with the iron likeness beside it. Proof. Promise. the frontier makes family — carved, at the ranch foreman's insistence, on the frame."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Sundown', 'text': "the ranch foreman keeps a room ready, and the kettle on, and the news of the Rio Bravo valley in letters. 'Come home when the trail allows,' every letter ends. The trail always allows, eventually.\n\nthe frontier makes family. In the Rio Bravo valley, on the trail, at sundown and sunup. The west remembers.\n\nSpring came, and Ada Coleman rode out with the thaw, a land deed's story told and retold in the saloon. the ranch foreman watched them go from the porch, and didn't wave — waving is for endings, and this wasn't one."}]}

